---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SEO from "../components/SEO.astro";
---
<BaseLayout>
  <SEO slot="head" title="About | Embedded & Circuits" description="Ajmal Ali is a high school student who taught himself electronics from scratch and writes up Arduino, ESP32, and sensor projects as he learns." path="/about/" />
  <div class="prose-container article-body" style="padding: var(--space-8) 0;">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      I'm Ajmal Ali. I'm in high school, and I teach myself electronics the slow
      way — with a breadboard, a laptop, and a lot of trial and error. I didn't
      grow up around this. Nobody in my family is an engineer. Everything on this
      site I learned from documentation, YouTube, and broken projects of my own.
    </p>

    <h2>How this started</h2>
    <p>
      The first thing I ever got to work was an LED lighting up. That is not an
      impressive sentence to read, and it took me far longer than it should have.
      I didn't have a breadboard, a resistor that was the right value, or anyone
      nearby to ask why the current-limiting resistor even mattered. I remember
      being genuinely confused that the light was dim, and later realising I'd
      picked a resistor more or less at random.
    </p>
    <p>
      What stuck wasn't the LED. It was realising I could get an idea working by
      myself. Nothing about that first circuit was taught to me. I read it, built
      it, got it wrong, read more, and tried again. That loop turned into an
      obsession.
    </p>
    <p>
      I didn't have anyone to ask, so I started writing down what I'd learned.
      These posts are those notes.
    </p>

    <h2>What I work with</h2>
    <p>
      Mostly the usual beginner-to-intermediate parts, which is exactly where the
      clear documentation and the real problems are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Arduino</strong> — mostly the UNO and Nano. My default board for
        anything I just want to light up, move, or beep at.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ESP32</strong> — this is where I spend most of my time. WiFi and
        Bluetooth, enough GPIO to be interesting, and a much more powerful chip to
        learn on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sensors and modules</strong> — ultrasonic (HC-SR04), temperature
        and humidity, relays, displays, and whatever else I can get working on a
        budget.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A breadboard and a lot of jumper wires</strong> — plus a
        multimeter, which I wish I'd owned a year earlier.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      My supply of boards and modules has grown faster than my knowledge, which is
      a problem I'd like to fix.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this blog exists</h2>
    <p>
      Two reasons, and they're both selfish.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first is that I kept losing the same fights. A sensor that worked on my
      desk and returned garbage on the other side of the room. A pin that silently
      did nothing because I'd forgotten it was an input pin. If I wrote it down once,
      I wouldn't have to solve it twice — and the writing forced me to actually
      understand the fix instead of just getting the light to turn on.
    </p>
    <p>
      The second is that when you're learning something alone, the tutorial either
      assumes you already know the one thing you're stuck on, or skips the three
      steps that actually went wrong. I lost real evenings to that. If someone else
      is stuck at 1 a.m. with a breadboard that won't cooperate, I'd rather they
      find the answer here than lose the night the way I did.
    </p>
    <p>
      I write about the mistakes more than the wins, because the mistakes are the
      part that was useful to me.
    </p>

    <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Wiring and interfacing guides for common sensors and modules</li>
      <li>Arduino and ESP32 project walkthroughs, including what went wrong</li>
      <li>Notes on debugging hardware and firmware that misbehave only sometimes</li>
      <li>The occasional post on the tooling and code around embedded work</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where this is going</h2>
    <p>
      I want to study robotics and hardware engineering properly. I know that
      "self-taught and motivated" is not a substitute for a degree, and I'm not
      treating it as one — this blog isn't standing in for a qualification, it's
      the work itself. It's the record of what I've actually built and understood,
      and if it's also useful to someone else, so much the better.
    </p>
    <p>
      I don't have all of this figured out yet. I don't fully understand C, I still
      wire things up backwards, and some of these posts are me working something
      out in public. I plan to keep going.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/contact/">Get in touch</a> if you spot an error, want to suggest a
      topic, or just want to say hello. Corrections are genuinely welcome — I'd
      rather be wrong in a post someone can correct than quietly wrong in my own
      notes.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>